WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.920 --> 00:00:03.010
JEN PERSON: Welcome to
"Who's [? There?", ?]

00:00:03.010 --> 00:00:04.230
A show about Cloud Identity.

00:00:04.230 --> 00:00:07.456
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:10.837 --> 00:00:12.670
In the last episode of
"Who's [? There?", ?]

00:00:12.670 --> 00:00:15.730
I talked about why Identity
Platform is awesome,

00:00:15.730 --> 00:00:18.610
and how to enable it
in your GCP project.

00:00:18.610 --> 00:00:21.760
Today I'm showing you how to
integrate Identity Platform

00:00:21.760 --> 00:00:24.370
into your Android app in Java.

00:00:24.370 --> 00:00:27.010
If you haven't enabled
Identity Platform yet,

00:00:27.010 --> 00:00:29.710
I recommend you check out the
last episode of "Who's There?"

00:00:29.710 --> 00:00:31.450
before proceeding.

00:00:31.450 --> 00:00:33.690
You also need an Android app.

00:00:33.690 --> 00:00:36.220
In Android Studio, start
with an existing app,

00:00:36.220 --> 00:00:37.820
or create a new one.

00:00:37.820 --> 00:00:40.480
And if Android doesn't
happen to be your jam,

00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:43.780
check out the Getting Started
videos for iOS and web,

00:00:43.780 --> 00:00:45.320
both linked below.

00:00:45.320 --> 00:00:47.780
All right, with all the
prerequisites out of the way,

00:00:47.780 --> 00:00:50.210
let's get started.

00:00:50.210 --> 00:00:52.150
In the Cloud console
for my project,

00:00:52.150 --> 00:00:55.450
I selected the
Identity Platform tab.

00:00:55.450 --> 00:00:58.550
I previously had an email
and password authentication,

00:00:58.550 --> 00:01:00.970
so if you haven't yet,
click Add a Provider

00:01:00.970 --> 00:01:03.380
and select Email Password.

00:01:03.380 --> 00:01:05.290
There are lots of other
providers to explore,

00:01:05.290 --> 00:01:08.710
but today, I'm just going
to stick with this one.

00:01:08.710 --> 00:01:11.080
On the Providers
page, you see a link

00:01:11.080 --> 00:01:14.590
in the upper-right for
Application Setup Details.

00:01:14.590 --> 00:01:18.400
This pops up a window with brief
getting started information.

00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:20.032
When I click the
Android tab, I see

00:01:20.032 --> 00:01:21.490
to get started with
Android, you'll

00:01:21.490 --> 00:01:24.250
need to register and add
Firebase configuration

00:01:24.250 --> 00:01:25.030
to your app.

00:01:25.030 --> 00:01:26.890
Learn More.

00:01:26.890 --> 00:01:29.500
I click Get Started with
Firebase, which takes me

00:01:29.500 --> 00:01:32.270
to an entirely new console.

00:01:32.270 --> 00:01:34.780
Some of the information is
the same as the Cloud Console,

00:01:34.780 --> 00:01:37.210
like project name
and ID, but then

00:01:37.210 --> 00:01:38.680
there are all
these new tabs down

00:01:38.680 --> 00:01:42.310
the side that say Develop,
Quality, Analytics, and Grow--

00:01:42.310 --> 00:01:46.780
and a big pop-up in the middle
asking about data sharing.

00:01:46.780 --> 00:01:50.247
If you've used Firebase before,
this probably looks familiar,

00:01:50.247 --> 00:01:52.330
but it's entirely possible
you've never even heard

00:01:52.330 --> 00:01:55.040
of Firebase, let alone used it.

00:01:55.040 --> 00:01:57.760
So let's talk about
what's going on here.

00:01:57.760 --> 00:02:00.760
Firebase is a suite of products
to help you build your app

00:02:00.760 --> 00:02:02.890
and grow your user base.

00:02:02.890 --> 00:02:05.680
It includes SDKs for
Objective-C and Swift

00:02:05.680 --> 00:02:09.770
for iOS, Java and Kotlin for
Android, JavaScript for web,

00:02:09.770 --> 00:02:12.970
as well as several web
frameworks like Angular.

00:02:12.970 --> 00:02:18.350
There are game-specific SDKs for
Unity in C++, and so much more.

00:02:18.350 --> 00:02:21.400
I can make a whole series
of videos just on Firebase,

00:02:21.400 --> 00:02:24.610
but fortunately, the Firebase
team has already done that.

00:02:24.610 --> 00:02:26.480
If you're interested
in finding out more,

00:02:26.480 --> 00:02:29.080
check out the documentation in
the Firebase YouTube channel,

00:02:29.080 --> 00:02:31.070
both linked below.

00:02:31.070 --> 00:02:33.670
So in many ways, Firebase
is the perfect complement

00:02:33.670 --> 00:02:34.900
to Google Cloud.

00:02:34.900 --> 00:02:37.600
Very broadly speaking,
Google Cloud products

00:02:37.600 --> 00:02:40.480
focus on server-side
solutions like App Engine,

00:02:40.480 --> 00:02:43.180
Compute Engine, Cloud
Functions, Kubernetes Engine,

00:02:43.180 --> 00:02:44.220
and Cloud Run.

00:02:44.220 --> 00:02:47.230
And Firebase products focus
on client-side solutions

00:02:47.230 --> 00:02:49.720
like authentication,
cloud messaging,

00:02:49.720 --> 00:02:52.665
cloud storage for
Firebase, and so on.

00:02:52.665 --> 00:02:54.040
And I admit that
it's challenging

00:02:54.040 --> 00:02:56.410
to pin down the relationship
between Google Cloud

00:02:56.410 --> 00:02:57.760
and Firebase.

00:02:57.760 --> 00:02:59.800
So for my oversimplified
explanation

00:02:59.800 --> 00:03:02.500
is that Firebase is a way
to access some Google Cloud

00:03:02.500 --> 00:03:05.890
Platform products from
a client application.

00:03:05.890 --> 00:03:08.080
Specifically, for
Identity Platform,

00:03:08.080 --> 00:03:10.060
this explanation works well.

00:03:10.060 --> 00:03:11.980
The Firebase Auth
SDKs enable you

00:03:11.980 --> 00:03:14.590
to access Identity
Platform functionality

00:03:14.590 --> 00:03:16.240
from your client app.

00:03:16.240 --> 00:03:21.160
With that in mind, let's head
back to that Firebase Console.

00:03:21.160 --> 00:03:22.960
The Firebase Console
automatically

00:03:22.960 --> 00:03:25.050
opened to our project.

00:03:25.050 --> 00:03:27.940
Notice this UI indicates
that Quick Start is already

00:03:27.940 --> 00:03:29.590
a Firebase project.

00:03:29.590 --> 00:03:32.710
When adding Identity Platform
to a Google Cloud project,

00:03:32.710 --> 00:03:35.910
a Firebase project is
automatically created.

00:03:35.910 --> 00:03:38.757
First, let's deal
with this pop-up.

00:03:38.757 --> 00:03:40.590
It's up to you whether
you want to check off

00:03:40.590 --> 00:03:44.190
to use the default settings
for Google Analytics Data,

00:03:44.190 --> 00:03:46.980
or just to select Do This Later.

00:03:46.980 --> 00:03:51.730
Some Firebase products depend
on analytics, like A/B testing,

00:03:51.730 --> 00:03:54.300
so if you decide to
incorporate other features,

00:03:54.300 --> 00:03:56.250
you'll probably
want to enable this.

00:03:56.250 --> 00:03:59.560
Again, it's entirely up to you.

00:03:59.560 --> 00:04:01.580
When you click on
the Project Settings,

00:04:01.580 --> 00:04:04.330
notice that the settings are
identical to the Cloud Quick

00:04:04.330 --> 00:04:05.710
Start project.

00:04:05.710 --> 00:04:07.870
Firebase has basically
added some more details

00:04:07.870 --> 00:04:10.330
and functionality to
the Cloud project,

00:04:10.330 --> 00:04:12.370
similar to when
you enable an API

00:04:12.370 --> 00:04:15.280
or add software from
the marketplace.

00:04:15.280 --> 00:04:18.440
Since you want to add Identity
Platform to an Android app,

00:04:18.440 --> 00:04:21.339
select Add App, Android.

00:04:21.339 --> 00:04:24.910
This gives you a window with
a series of steps to follow.

00:04:24.910 --> 00:04:27.460
Now, keep in mind when you
pick the Android package name,

00:04:27.460 --> 00:04:30.950
it must match the package
name of your Android app.

00:04:30.950 --> 00:04:33.650
The app nickname is
totally up to you.

00:04:33.650 --> 00:04:36.190
You can leave it
blank if you prefer.

00:04:36.190 --> 00:04:38.170
I don't provide the
SHA-1 certificate,

00:04:38.170 --> 00:04:40.480
since I'm not using any of
the products for which it

00:04:40.480 --> 00:04:41.780
is required.

00:04:41.780 --> 00:04:45.280
But if you plan on implementing
Google Sign In or Phone Number

00:04:45.280 --> 00:04:48.700
Sign [? In and ?] [? Auth, ?]
feel free to add the SHA-1 here

00:04:48.700 --> 00:04:52.610
before creating [? an app. ?]
You can also add it later.

00:04:52.610 --> 00:04:56.600
Now you are prompted to download
a Google Services JSON file.

00:04:56.600 --> 00:05:00.200
This file informs your Android
app about the Firebase app

00:05:00.200 --> 00:05:03.750
so you can interface
with Firebase products.

00:05:03.750 --> 00:05:07.500
Once downloaded, add this
to your Android project.

00:05:07.500 --> 00:05:10.830
Click Next in the console, and
it gives you the next step.

00:05:10.830 --> 00:05:14.760
Add the gradle dependencies
required to use Firebase.

00:05:14.760 --> 00:05:17.070
In addition to the
gradle files required

00:05:17.070 --> 00:05:19.560
to build a Firebase
app, you also

00:05:19.560 --> 00:05:23.670
need specific gradle file
to use Firebase Auth.

00:05:23.670 --> 00:05:28.170
Add the following project level
and app level dependencies.

00:05:28.170 --> 00:05:31.480
You can see them here.

00:05:31.480 --> 00:05:33.880
In your app in Android
Studio, declare

00:05:33.880 --> 00:05:36.220
an instance of Firebase Auth.

00:05:36.220 --> 00:05:38.770
OK, now you can use
the Firebase Auth

00:05:38.770 --> 00:05:42.370
SDK to sign up, log
in, log out, and get

00:05:42.370 --> 00:05:46.870
user info about an
Identity Platform user.

00:05:46.870 --> 00:05:48.700
Identity Platform
gives you access

00:05:48.700 --> 00:05:50.960
to many different
log in providers,

00:05:50.960 --> 00:05:54.020
including federated identity
providers like Google,

00:05:54.020 --> 00:05:55.720
Facebook, and Twitter.

00:05:55.720 --> 00:05:58.750
You can enable several
log in methods for an app,

00:05:58.750 --> 00:06:00.400
allowing your users
to authenticate

00:06:00.400 --> 00:06:02.650
the way that they prefer.

00:06:02.650 --> 00:06:05.500
Some log in methods may
require additional steps,

00:06:05.500 --> 00:06:07.660
which are listed
in the console when

00:06:07.660 --> 00:06:09.880
you enable that log in method.

00:06:09.880 --> 00:06:12.070
See the documentation
below for information

00:06:12.070 --> 00:06:14.950
on how to implement
other log in providers.

00:06:14.950 --> 00:06:18.280
For now, let's get back to
implementing email and password

00:06:18.280 --> 00:06:20.090
log in.

00:06:20.090 --> 00:06:22.480
In the Cloud Console,
create a fake user

00:06:22.480 --> 00:06:25.540
with a test email
and a test password.

00:06:25.540 --> 00:06:27.940
Of course, you can create
a new user from the client,

00:06:27.940 --> 00:06:30.130
but this is a good
way to get your app up

00:06:30.130 --> 00:06:31.960
and running quickly.

00:06:31.960 --> 00:06:33.820
See how to create a
user in your Android

00:06:33.820 --> 00:06:36.770
app following the link below.

00:06:36.770 --> 00:06:40.730
In onStart, call a
function called logIn.

00:06:40.730 --> 00:06:43.790
This function calls the
Firebase Auth method sign

00:06:43.790 --> 00:06:46.040
in with email and
password, passing

00:06:46.040 --> 00:06:50.090
the email and password you
created in the Cloud Console.

00:06:50.090 --> 00:06:51.890
When the sign in
function completes,

00:06:51.890 --> 00:06:53.750
the onComplete
listener is called,

00:06:53.750 --> 00:06:56.180
passing the Auth result task.

00:06:56.180 --> 00:06:59.160
If the task is successful,
then you are signed in.

00:06:59.160 --> 00:07:02.270
I've added a log
[? and toast ?] to reflect this.

00:07:02.270 --> 00:07:04.940
If the sign in fails, I
display this as well--

00:07:04.940 --> 00:07:07.310
just for our
reference right now.

00:07:07.310 --> 00:07:10.400
So it's clear that my example
thus far is not a complete auth

00:07:10.400 --> 00:07:11.540
solution.

00:07:11.540 --> 00:07:14.090
You need a way for users
to create accounts, handle

00:07:14.090 --> 00:07:16.790
password resets, and log out.

00:07:16.790 --> 00:07:20.390
Each of these functions needs
some UI development as well.

00:07:20.390 --> 00:07:23.870
If you prefer, you can write
all this functionality yourself

00:07:23.870 --> 00:07:26.630
using the Firebase
guides as reference.

00:07:26.630 --> 00:07:29.300
But you also have the option
to use the Firebase Auth UI

00:07:29.300 --> 00:07:30.590
module.

00:07:30.590 --> 00:07:35.180
This open source UI was created
by Auth specialists at Google.

00:07:35.180 --> 00:07:36.770
It includes all
the views you would

00:07:36.770 --> 00:07:38.810
need to implement
account creation,

00:07:38.810 --> 00:07:43.010
log in, log out, and
password reset functionality.

00:07:43.010 --> 00:07:46.850
You can incorporate Auth UI
in just a few lines of code.

00:07:46.850 --> 00:07:49.490
Personally, whenever
I start a new project,

00:07:49.490 --> 00:07:53.360
I use Firebase UI so I can focus
on the key features of my app,

00:07:53.360 --> 00:07:56.330
while still protecting data
through authentication.

00:07:56.330 --> 00:07:59.930
But whether you make your
own UI or use Firebase UI,

00:07:59.930 --> 00:08:02.390
you will very likely need
to know the auth status

00:08:02.390 --> 00:08:05.180
before performing
any app actions.

00:08:05.180 --> 00:08:08.330
For example, you want to check
if there is a user logged in

00:08:08.330 --> 00:08:10.640
before writing to a database.

00:08:10.640 --> 00:08:13.900
That's where getting the
current user comes into play.

00:08:13.900 --> 00:08:17.490
Let's look at how to
implement that functionality.

00:08:17.490 --> 00:08:19.800
To get the currently
signed-in user,

00:08:19.800 --> 00:08:24.223
call getCurrentUser on your
Firebase Auth instance.

00:08:24.223 --> 00:08:26.640
You may have noticed that this
function was already called

00:08:26.640 --> 00:08:28.950
in the onSuccess listener.

00:08:28.950 --> 00:08:31.050
If the appearance of
your UI depends upon

00:08:31.050 --> 00:08:35.370
whether there is a current user,
you can call getCurrentUser

00:08:35.370 --> 00:08:37.659
in onStart.

00:08:37.659 --> 00:08:41.140
I put the code to log out in
this separate function called

00:08:41.140 --> 00:08:42.580
logOut.

00:08:42.580 --> 00:08:44.350
I'm going to call this
function at the end

00:08:44.350 --> 00:08:48.380
of the onSuccess listener if
the log in was successful.

00:08:48.380 --> 00:08:52.220
Of course, it's unusual to log
in only to log out immediately,

00:08:52.220 --> 00:08:54.420
but this is purely for
the sake of example,

00:08:54.420 --> 00:08:57.450
so you can see the
log out function run.

00:08:57.450 --> 00:09:01.260
OK, I build and run the
app, and the console

00:09:01.260 --> 00:09:06.720
logs show that the user has
logged in, and then logged out.

00:09:06.720 --> 00:09:08.100
Congratulations.

00:09:08.100 --> 00:09:11.310
You just added Identity
Platform to an Android app.

00:09:11.310 --> 00:09:14.250
Ready to see what else
Identity Platform can do?

00:09:14.250 --> 00:09:17.310
Check out the Firebase Auth
Android guides, Auth Quick

00:09:17.310 --> 00:09:20.550
Start Code, and Firebase
YouTube channel,

00:09:20.550 --> 00:09:23.100
and subscribe to the Google
Cloud Platform channel

00:09:23.100 --> 00:09:26.440
so you can be the first to
know about new videos in the

00:09:26.440 --> 00:09:28.360
"Who's There?" series.

00:09:28.360 --> 00:09:32.180
Thanks for watching, and I'll
see you on a future episode.

